    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Modified_Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    nf = 5\n    nc = 11\n    facility_coords = [(28, 34), (16, 94), (11, 57), (6, 61), (12, 73)]\n    customer_coords = [(58, 31), (34, 37), (84, 37), (50, 17), (61, 7), (37, 81), (60, 76), (3, 45), (77, 42), (35, 63), (42, 96)]\n    capacities = [195.0, 131.0, 137.0, 150.0, 198.0]\n    demands = [25.0, 20.0, 19.0, 31.0, 25.0, 40.0, 25.0, 40.0, 42.0, 35.0, 34.0]\n    fixed_costs = [486.0, 173.0, 366.0, 140.0, 218.0]\n    rate = 0.5\n\n    # Compute distances\n    dist = {}\n    for i in range(nf):\n        for j in range(nc):\n            dx = facility_coords[i][0] - customer_coords[j][0]\n            dy = facility_coords[i][1] - customer_coords[j][1]\n            dist[(i,j)] = math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy)\n\n    # Variables\n    open_f = {i: m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f'Open_F{i}') for i in range(nf)}\n    ship = {(i,j): m.addVar(lb=0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f'Ship_F{i}_C{j}')\n            for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc)}\n\n    # Objective\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i]*open_f[i] for i in range(nf))\n    obj += quicksum(dist[(i,j)]*rate*ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf) for j in range(nc))\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction\n    for j in range(nc):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for i in range(nf)) >= demands[j])\n\n    # Capacity linking\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= capacities[i]*open_f[i])\n\n    # Policy: no facility serves > 51% of total demand\n    total_demand = 336.0\n    for i in range(nf):\n        m.addConstr(quicksum(ship[(i,j)] for j in range(nc)) <= 0.51*total_demand)\n\n    # Policy: F1 requires F3\n    m.addConstr(open_f[1] <= open_f[3])\n\n    # Policy: at most 4 facilities open\n    m.addConstr(quicksum(open_f[i] for i in range(nf)) <= 4)\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: Executive Leadership Team\nFrom: Supply Chain Consultant\nSubject: Facility Opening and Customer Allocation Plan to Minimize Total Distribution Cost\n\nPurpose\nThis memo outlines the decision framework to determine which distribution centers (DCs) to open and how to allocate customer shipments in order to minimize total network cost while meeting service obligations and complying with established operating policies.\n\nDecision Context\nWe are evaluating a set of candidate DC locations and a set of customer locations, each specified with map coordinates. Each candidate DC has a fixed cost to operate and a capacity limit, and each customer has a defined demand requirement. Transportation cost will be calculated based on the distance between DCs and customers and the volume shipped, using the company\u2019s standard transport rate (see Annex). The decision is twofold: which DCs to activate, and how to route shipments from the opened DCs to fully satisfy all customer demand.\n\nBusiness Requirements\nService and operations\n- All customer demand must be fully satisfied; partial fulfillment or backorders are not permitted in the plan.\n- Shipments may originate only from DCs we elect to open; non\u2011operational sites may not dispatch product.\n- No DC may ship more than its available capacity; all allocations must respect each facility\u2019s rated throughput.\n- Total network cost comprises:\n  - The fixed operating cost for each DC that is opened; and\n  - The variable freight cost for every shipment, determined by distance and volume at the company\u2019s approved transport rate (see Annex). Distances will be based on the provided map coordinates.\n\nMandatory company policies\n- Over\u2011reliance guardrail: To maintain resilience and avoid over\u2011dependence on a single node, no DC may be planned to fulfill more than 51% of total system demand. This cap applies to the aggregate volume a single DC serves across all customers.\n- Hub dependency: The candidate site designated F1 depends on the regional hub designated F3. If F1 is activated, F3 must also be activated concurrently to provide the required regional support.\n- Management span\u2011of\u2011control: Due to oversight capacity, the company will operate at most 4 DCs at any given time. The recommended footprint must therefore open no more than four sites.\n\nGoal\nIdentify the combination of DCs to open and the allocation of customer shipments that satisfies all demand and capacity requirements, adheres to the above policies, and yields the lowest total cost across fixed DC costs and transportation expenses.\n\nApproach\n- Evaluate feasible combinations of up to four DCs, ensuring the F1\u2013F3 dependency is honored whenever F1 is selected.\n- For each feasible footprint, allocate shipments from opened DCs to customers to fully meet demand while:\n  - Staying within each DC\u2019s capacity,\n  - Respecting the 51% share cap on any single DC\u2019s contribution to total system demand, and\n  - Applying transportation costs based on distance and volume at the standard transport rate.\n- Where beneficial and compliant with policies, customer demand may be split across multiple DCs to lower total cost and improve capacity utilization.\n- Compare scenarios on total cost and operational robustness; provide a recommended footprint and shipment plan, plus one or two viable alternates as risk\u2011mitigated options.\n\nDeliverables\n- Recommended set of DCs to open (up to four), with rationale tied to cost and resilience.\n- Customer\u2011to\u2011DC shipment plan that fully satisfies demand, including volumes, utilization by DC, and confirmation that the 51% cap is not exceeded.\n- Cost breakdown showing fixed DC costs and variable transport costs at the standard rate.\n- Sensitivity notes on key drivers (e.g., shifts in demand or transport rate) and how the recommendation would adapt.\n\nAnnex: Data Inputs\n- Candidate facility coordinates: Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (28, 34)\n  - F1: (16, 94)\n  - F2: (11, 57)\n  - F3: (6, 61)\n  - F4: (12, 73)\n- Customer coordinates: Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (58, 31)\n  - C1: (34, 37)\n  - C2: (84, 37)\n  - C3: (50, 17)\n  - C4: (61, 7)\n  - C5: (37, 81)\n  - C6: (60, 76)\n  - C7: (3, 45)\n  - C8: (77, 42)\n  - C9: (35, 63)\n  - C10: (42, 96)\n- Facility capacities (units): [195.0, 131.0, 137.0, 150.0, 198.0]\n- Customer demands (units): [25.0, 20.0, 19.0, 31.0, 25.0, 40.0, 25.0, 40.0, 42.0, 35.0, 34.0]\n- Fixed operating costs by facility: [486.0, 173.0, 366.0, 140.0, 218.0]\n- Transport rate (cost per km per unit): 0.5",
    "LLM_description_template": "To: Executive Leadership Team\nFrom: Supply Chain Consultant\nSubject: Facility Opening and Customer Allocation Plan to Minimize Total Distribution Cost\n\nPurpose\nThis memo outlines the decision framework to determine which distribution centers (DCs) to open and how to allocate customer shipments in order to minimize total network cost while meeting service obligations and complying with established operating policies.\n\nDecision Context\nWe are evaluating a set of candidate DC locations and a set of customer locations, each specified with map coordinates. Each candidate DC has a fixed cost to operate and a capacity limit, and each customer has a defined demand requirement. Transportation cost will be calculated based on the distance between DCs and customers and the volume shipped, using the company\u2019s standard transport rate (see Annex). The decision is twofold: which DCs to activate, and how to route shipments from the opened DCs to fully satisfy all customer demand.\n\nBusiness Requirements\nService and operations\n- All customer demand must be fully satisfied; partial fulfillment or backorders are not permitted in the plan.\n- Shipments may originate only from DCs we elect to open; non\u2011operational sites may not dispatch product.\n- No DC may ship more than its available capacity; all allocations must respect each facility\u2019s rated throughput.\n- Total network cost comprises:\n  - The fixed operating cost for each DC that is opened; and\n  - The variable freight cost for every shipment, determined by distance and volume at the company\u2019s approved transport rate (see Annex). Distances will be based on the provided map coordinates.\n\nMandatory company policies\n- Over\u2011reliance guardrail: To maintain resilience and avoid over\u2011dependence on a single node, no DC may be planned to fulfill more than 51% of total system demand. This cap applies to the aggregate volume a single DC serves across all customers.\n- Hub dependency: The candidate site designated F1 depends on the regional hub designated F3. If F1 is activated, F3 must also be activated concurrently to provide the required regional support.\n- Management span\u2011of\u2011control: Due to oversight capacity, the company will operate at most 4 DCs at any given time. The recommended footprint must therefore open no more than four sites.\n\nGoal\nIdentify the combination of DCs to open and the allocation of customer shipments that satisfies all demand and capacity requirements, adheres to the above policies, and yields the lowest total cost across fixed DC costs and transportation expenses.\n\nApproach\n- Evaluate feasible combinations of up to four DCs, ensuring the F1\u2013F3 dependency is honored whenever F1 is selected.\n- For each feasible footprint, allocate shipments from opened DCs to customers to fully meet demand while:\n  - Staying within each DC\u2019s capacity,\n  - Respecting the 51% share cap on any single DC\u2019s contribution to total system demand, and\n  - Applying transportation costs based on distance and volume at the standard transport rate.\n- Where beneficial and compliant with policies, customer demand may be split across multiple DCs to lower total cost and improve capacity utilization.\n- Compare scenarios on total cost and operational robustness; provide a recommended footprint and shipment plan, plus one or two viable alternates as risk\u2011mitigated options.\n\nDeliverables\n- Recommended set of DCs to open (up to four), with rationale tied to cost and resilience.\n- Customer\u2011to\u2011DC shipment plan that fully satisfies demand, including volumes, utilization by DC, and confirmation that the 51% cap is not exceeded.\n- Cost breakdown showing fixed DC costs and variable transport costs at the standard rate.\n- Sensitivity notes on key drivers (e.g., shifts in demand or transport rate) and how the recommendation would adapt.\n\nAnnex: Data Inputs\n- Candidate facility coordinates: {FACILITY_COORDINATES}\n- Customer coordinates: {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}\n- Facility capacities (units): {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}\n- Customer demands (units): {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}\n- Fixed operating costs by facility: {FIXED_COSTS}\n- Transport rate (cost per km per unit): {TRANSPORT_RATE}"
